================================================================
* METHODOLOGY
================================================================
Comparative architecture analysis + simulation + real-world testbed,
in three stages:

1. Architecture scenario comparison (Section 3):
   Four 5G defense network scenarios were compared along a spectrum of
   commercial-vs-defense component sharing:
   - 2.A: Fully isolated defense network — dedicated gNB, CP, UP.
     Max security, high cost, limited coverage.
   - 2.B: Shared commercial gNB + CN CP, with a defense-owned UP.
     Lower cost, but subscriber privacy/location data is exposed to the
     commercial CP.
   - 2.C: Network-slicing approach — commercial and defense traffic
     coexist on shared infrastructure, logically separated by slice.
     Low cost/flexible, but slicing standardization is immature and risk
     of unauthorized cross-slice access exists without strong encryption.
   - 2.D (chosen approach): Commercial gNB + military-owned CN (CP+UP),
     distinguished via a dedicated military PLMN ID. Broad coverage and
     cost efficiency from reusing commercial gNB/frequency bands, while
     keeping core network control (and thus security) in military hands.
     Requires commercial gNB/operator support for routing the military
     PLMN ID.

2. Proposed ZT architecture design (Section 4):
   - Layer 2.D with an SDP-based ZT layer.
   - The "5G Military ZT Manager" plays the SDP Controller role.
   - The military CN plays the SDP Accepting Host (AH).
   - The UE and the private-network application server both act as SDP
     Initiating Hosts (IH).
   - Defined a full communication flow (Fig. 4): ZT-network preparation →
     standard 5G-based session establishment (PLMN selection, 5G auth,
     5G data channel) → SDP authentication/authorization exchange between
     UE, CN, and ZT manager → establishment of the SDP data channel
     between UE and the private-network server.

3. Empirical validation:
   a. Simulation (Section 5): built a virtual 5G environment using
      Open5GS (core network) and UERANSIM (UE/RAN), across 6 Ubuntu 22.04
      VMs. SDP components implemented in C++ (IH), Python (AH), and C++
      with a Spring/Java web GUI (ZT manager/controller). Two UEs were
      simulated: one as a legitimate client, one as an attacker performing
      DoS (high-volume TCP flooding) and IP-scanning (nmap) attacks.
      Metrics: connection setup time, average throughput, with/without SDP,
      with/without attack — benchmarked against a prior study [32].
   b. Real-world testbed (Section 6): a physical rig — mini-PC + Quectel
      RM500Q-GL 5G modem as UE, a USRP B210 SDR running OpenAirInterface
      as the gNB, and separate mini-PCs for the CN (OAI), the 5G Military
      ZT manager, and a web server. Verified end-to-end SDP authentication
      behavior (UE blocked from the server pre-authentication; UPF
      iptables updated post-authentication to allow access) and captured
      screenshots/logs of the SDP IH/AH/controller in action.

