* SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS
================================================================
Simulation environment (6 VMs, Ubuntu 22.04, subnet 192.168.56.x):
  - VM1: UERANSIM UE (SDP IH) — simulates UE0 (attacker) and UE1 (client)
  - VM2: UERANSIM RAN (gNB)
  - VM3: Open5GS CN Control Plane — AMF, SMF (SDP AH)
  - VM4: Open5GS CN User Plane — UPF (SDP AH)
  - VM5: 5G Military ZT manager (SDP controller) — web GUI via Spring/Java
  - VM6: Application/iPerf server (SDP IH)
  Software: Open5GS (core network), UERANSIM (UE/RAN simulator), SDP IH in
  C++, SDP AH in Python, ZT manager core in C++ with Java/Spring GUI,
  nmap for scanning-attack simulation, iPerf for throughput testing.

Real-world testbed:
  - UE: mini-PC + Quectel RM500Q-GL 5G modem, Windows 10, IP 12.1.1.2
    (SDP IH)
  - gNB: USRP B210 SDR running OpenAirInterface (OAI) 5G NR SA
  - CN: mini-PC running OpenAirInterface CN, Ubuntu 22.04, IP
    192.168.0.10 (SDP AH)
  - 5G Military ZT manager: separate host, IP 192.168.0.12 (SDP
    controller)
  - Web/application server: IP 192.168.0.14 (SDP IH)
  - Physical setup housed in a shielded rack ("shield box") with SDR, ZT
    manager, CN, web server, and 5G modem/UE modules.

================================================================
* RESULTS
================================================================
Connection time (avg. of 10 runs):
  - Without SDP: 0.297 s (this study) vs. 0.09 s reported in [32]
    (difference attributed to wireless vs. wired test environments).
  - With SDP: 0.786 s (this study) vs. 1.02 s in [32].
  - Conclusion: SDP adds modest connection-time overhead but remains
    practical for a 5G wireless environment.

Average throughput, no attack:
  - Without SDP: 299.7 Mbit/s (baseline, ~300 Mbit/s stable).
  - With SDP: 197.6 Mbit/s (reduction attributed to overhead of SDP
    control/data channels and per-second keep-alive messages).

Average throughput, under DoS attack (single attacker node):
  - Without SDP: dropped to 145.9 Mbit/s (~50% of normal capacity, i.e.
    roughly half the baseline throughput).
  - With SDP: maintained 194.3 Mbit/s (above 65% of baseline), showing
    SDP substantially mitigates the DoS impact.
  - Comparison to [32]: their DDoS test (multiple attacker nodes) showed
    a much larger drop (1.51 Gbit/s -> 6.86 Mbit/s), reflecting a more
    extreme multi-attacker scenario versus this paper's single-attacker
    DoS case.

IP scanning attack (nmap) results:
  - Without SDP: all 7 hosts on the subnet detected/enumerated (~66.66
    seconds scan), exposing core components (ZT manager, CN CP, UPF, and
    other devices) to the attacker.
  - With SDP: only 2 hosts detected (ZT manager and UPF), in ~17.65
    seconds — CN CP, application server, and other devices were hidden
    from the scan, since the UPF (as SDP AH) blocks unauthenticated
    devices from discovering/reaching them.

Real-world testbed verification:
  - Before SDP authentication: UE has an active 5G connection but cannot
    reach the private web server (UPF iptables only permit traffic to the
    ZT manager IP).
  - After SDP authentication: UPF iptables update to add an "anywhere"
    ACCEPT rule for the authenticated UE's IP, and the UE successfully
    accesses the web server.
  - Confirms that SDP-based ZT blocks unauthorized access even when a
    valid 5G radio link already exists.

